  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nConditions are heating up outside with temperatures in the 90s and \ntriple digits and heat index values ranging from around 106 degrees \nand around 120 degrees. Temperatures are expected to continue to \nheat up this afternoon with highs ranging from the mid 90s to around \n105 degrees. A few isolated areas, mainly east of Highway 281, may \nexperience heat index values between 115 and 120 degrees. The whole \narea is in an Extreme Heat Warning. Winds are expected to increase a \nbit out of the south this afternoon but will decrease this evening. \nAn approaching boundary from the north this evening may result in \nshowers and thunderstorms (primarily along and north of Highway 6 in \nNebraska). The best chances (15% to 20%) will be north and west of \nthe Tri-Cities area between 6 PM and 10 PM. Some of these storms \nhave the potential to be strong to severe with wind gusts up to \naround 60 MPH being the main hazard. The storms are expected to \nweaken as they move southward. The Tri-Cities area may experience a \nfew showers and possibly a weak thunderstorm around 10 to 11 PM. Low \ntemperatures tonight will only cool to around the mid 70s across \nsouth central Nebraska and the upper 70s to low 80s in north central \nKansas. \n\nA north to northeast wind shift will begin moving into the area on \nMonday. This boundary does not appear like it will have much impact \non temperatures, but models are showing an increase of moisture \nbehind the boundary. Winds are expected to increase out of the \nnortheast behind the boundary which may help to mix out some of the \nlow-level moisture. There are uncertainties as to how much of an \ninfluence this boundary will have on temperatures, moisture, and \ncloud cover for Monday. There is higher confidence across north \ncentral Kansas for the combination of temperatures (around 100 to \n106 degrees) and moisture to produce heat index values near or above \n110 degrees for Monday. For this reason, the Heat Advisory across \nthis area was upgraded to an Extreme Heat Warning which is in effect \nuntil 10 PM Monday. There is lower confidence (due to the above \nmentioned factors) across south central and central Nebraska so \nthose areas were not upgraded at this time. Although, the Nebraska \ncounties bordering Kansas have the highest likelihood of \nreaching Extreme Heat criteria on Monday and there is fairly \nhigh confidence (around 70%) that some areas in these counties \nmay have heat index values near/around 110 degrees on Monday. \nHeat index values across our Nebraska counties are expected to \nrange from around 101 in Ord to around 106 to 110 degrees across\nthe far southern row of counties. Showers and thunderstorms \nwill be possible (up to around a 50% chance) Monday evening and \nnight as an upper disturbance moves overhead from the west. The \nhighest chances will be across western portions of the area \n(mainly west of the Tri-Cities area). Some of these storms may \nbecome strong to marginally severe with wind gusts up to around \n60 MPH being the main threat. The showers and storms will move \neastward as the night continues. \n\nCooler temperatures are expected beginning Tuesday (highs in the \nupper 80s to low/mid 90s) as a surface high moves across the area. \nHigh temperatures in the 80s and 90s will continue through the end \nof the week. Low temperatures will be in the 60s and 70s. Various \nrain and storm chances will continue Tuesday through Thursday night \nas upper disturbances move over the area.",
